<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>{12}</m> workers lay <m>{360}</m> m of drainage pipe in <m>{10}</m> days. How many days would <m>{15}</m> workers take to lay <m>{540}</m> m, working at the same rate? Use the ratio method.</p><p>(a) The change in length multiplies the number of days by <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/>  </p><p>(b) The change in the number of workers multiplies the number of days by <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/>  </p><p>(c) Days needed: <fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/> days  </p><p>Give (a) and (b) as fractions, for example <c>3/4</c>, or as decimals correct to 3 decimal places; give (c) exactly or correct to 1 decimal place.</p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Change one quantity at a time and ask whether the answer gets bigger or smaller.</p><p>(a) Length: <m>{360}</m> m to <m>{540}</m> m. More pipe needs more days, so the factor is greater than 1: <m>\dfrac{ {540} }{ {360} } = {\frac{3}{2}}</m>.</p><p>(b) Workers: <m>{12}</m> to <m>{15}</m>. More workers need fewer days, so the factor is less than 1: <m>\dfrac{ {12} }{ {15} } = {\frac{4}{5}}</m>.</p><p>(c) Days <m>= {10} \times {\frac{3}{2}} \times {\frac{4}{5}} = {12}</m>.</p><p>Check by the unitary method: the job rate is <m>{360} \div ({12} \times {10}) = {3}</m> m per worker-day, and <m>{540} \div ({15} \times {3}) = {12}</m> days.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{3}{2}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{4}{5}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>12</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>